  "result": "I wrote the revised record to `record.jsonl`. Only three scenarios changed; the rule itself and every mapping stay the same. The record hasn't been through the gate yet, so it's still unconfirmed that the three changed scenarios now reach their verdicts.\n\n- **`idle`**: the overtaken car is gone; the subject now drives alone on the one-lane road and steers right. Under this run's reading a car can't be decided outside the electric-bicycle and electric-scooter definitions, so it always came out unknown. With no other road user, the rule decides \"not applicable\".\n- **`blind`**: the source now hides the lanes' direction of travel (`st.lane_direction`) instead of headings (`kin.heading`). Hiding headings also hid bearing, same-direction and overlap, so the gate saw unknowns it didn't expect. Lane direction is read only by the \"heading right of the lane\" check in the obligation. With the bicycle still beside the car, \"safely clear\" is decided false, so the only reason for unknown is the hidden lane direction.\n- **`branch_1`**: the overtaken rider is now a bicycle rather than an electric scooter. The run's reading leaves the electric-scooter class unresolved under its own definition, so a scooter scene can never come out \"violated\". The case it tests is unchanged: the pass is completed and the car steers back right with the gap below the bicycle's speed times the dry headway.\n\n**Not tested:** no scenario now exercises an electric scooter, because the run's reading leaves the electric-scooter class unresolved under its own definition (NRS 484B.018), so no scooter scene can be decided. That classification is the reading's, not something this record can fix.\n\nThe five \"Except as otherwise provided in NRS 484B.213 and 484B.217\" compositions are kept as planned, the same as on the two sibling nodes, which were accepted. The coder didn't write them into the rule, since the authorization they suspend (b12) is kept only as a residue with no effect on